  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 217 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nA potent upper-level trough will slowly propagate eastward across \nthe central CONUS Monday through Wednesday. Although there are \nlingering differences across model guidance on the exact structure \nand speed of the trough, there are still projections of anomalous, \ndeep meridional flow with anomalous PW around the 99th \nclimatological percentile and a slow-moving front all aligning to \nprovide an opportunity for widespread, beneficial rainfall across \nthe CWA. NBM probabilities of 1\"+ of rainfall have been consistently \naround 50 to 80 percent, highest in central and northeastern MO. \nEnsemble model membership generally agrees on most of this rain \nfalling in the Monday evening-through-Wednesday morning timeframe. \nThe only impact from this system may just be beneficial rainfall \nwith present indications that instability will be limited enough to \npreclude a clear heavy rainfall or severe weather threat with poor \nmid-level lapse rates from widespread clouds and precipitation along \nwith occlusion tending to \"pinch off\" the unstable warm sector. \nStill, strong deep-layer wind shear will likely be present, leaving \ninstability trends worth monitoring going forward. NBM interquartile \ntemperature ranges expand to around 10 F by next Thursday, spanning \nbetween continued above average temperatures and cooler temperatures \nnear average. This spread is attributed to uncertainties in the \ncharacteristics of the post-frontal airmass including the structure \nof the trough impacting the airmass' origin.",
